WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.012
[MUSIC]

00:00:06.012 --> 00:00:11.950
It's late 2008 and I'm in Kurdistan, Iraq
for a political science field trip.

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:16.640
We're driving on a long drive back to the
capital one afternoon

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:21.050
when we stop to stretch our legs at the
base of a gently sloping grassy hill.

00:00:21.050 --> 00:00:23.600
And as I get out of the bus, I look around
to see

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:25.995
if there's a good spot to have that
granola bar I've been saving.

00:00:25.995 --> 00:00:29.030
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And something catches my eye.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:34.770
Oozing out from under the rocks on the
hill are several foot wide streams

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.650
of a thick, brown, mud-like substance.

00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:42.585
And as I approach it to take a closer
look, our guide, as if he's read my mind,

00:00:42.585 --> 00:00:44.230
shouts, don't eat that!

00:00:45.490 --> 00:00:46.110
That's oil.

00:00:48.210 --> 00:00:49.080
My first reaction?

00:00:50.350 --> 00:00:51.830
Disgust.

00:00:51.830 --> 00:00:54.060
I was a student of environmental risk,

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.520
active in international climate change
conferences.

00:00:56.520 --> 00:01:00.305
I knew for a fact that oil was bad.

00:01:00.305 --> 00:01:03.130
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:07.770
&gt;&gt; But as I stared at this foreign brown
substance, fascination took over.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:11.350
Isn't oil black?

00:01:11.350 --> 00:01:12.310
What was it doing here?

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.179
Had an accident happened?

00:01:16.179 --> 00:01:20.909
Seeing this natural oil seep made me
realize that I was completely out of touch

00:01:20.909 --> 00:01:25.565
with oil, and from this moment forward,
oil, something that had seemed so

00:01:25.565 --> 00:01:29.595
clearly black and white to me took on many
shades of well, brown.

00:01:29.595 --> 00:01:30.238
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:01:30.238 --> 00:01:33.050
&gt;&gt; Now think back to the first time that
you saw oil.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:40.700
I bet that it was in a leak of some sort
and that it was bad news.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:43.730
It might have been motor oil, dripping out
from under your car.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:46.080
More likely though, it was on the news.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.770
Oil spilling out the side of a tanker.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:54.560
It's downright devastating what oil can do
to the environment isn't it?

00:01:54.560 --> 00:01:58.310
Those images of blackened beaches,
wildlife drenched in the stuff, and

00:01:58.310 --> 00:01:59.270
battling to breathe through it.

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:04.442
I was horrified to see how in Nigeria,
forests, rivers,

00:02:04.442 --> 00:02:09.359
livelihoods of thousands have been
poisoned by leaks in over 4,000 miles of

00:02:09.359 --> 00:02:12.054
pipelines crisscrossing the Niger Delta.

00:02:12.054 --> 00:02:17.980
But perhaps freshest in our minds is the
BP oil spill from 2010 that sent over 3.2

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:23.763
million barrels gushing out into the Gulf
of Mexico over the period of three months.

00:02:23.763 --> 00:02:29.210
Now 3.2 million barrels, that's 130
million gallons.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:30.860
I'm gonna try and put that in perspective.

00:02:31.980 --> 00:02:34.510
That's enough for you guys seated in the
back two rows

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:40.070
to get three Starbucks grandes everyday
for the rest of your long lives.

00:02:40.070 --> 00:02:43.187
And pass that gift card down for 1,000
generations.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:50.220
That's a lot, that a more bit a lot of
coffee.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:51.951
Now, fast forward a few years, and

00:02:51.951 --> 00:02:54.679
the student of environmental risk is now a
consultant.

00:02:54.679 --> 00:02:56.420
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] ,.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.330
&gt;&gt; Often, often for the oil and gas
industry.

00:03:01.060 --> 00:03:05.350
Now in the eyes of many Californians and
certainly many students here working with

00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:10.310
or for big oil is considered the single
most uncoolest job you could have.

00:03:10.310 --> 00:03:15.060
It's really the antithesis to being the
coveted tech entrepreneur but

00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:15.909
it's not just uncool.

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:18.140
It's seen as deplorable.

00:03:19.470 --> 00:03:22.430
Total strangers will take it upon
themselves to educate me about my

00:03:22.430 --> 00:03:23.880
malevolence and that of my colleagues.

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:29.710
Well meaning friends and family will say
is that ethical?

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:31.090
Shouldn't we get you out of there?

00:03:32.390 --> 00:03:35.220
As if I need rescuing because I've clearly
forgotten

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.239
to factor in morality into my daily
decisions.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:42.090
And on the one hand, I really do
appreciate the honesty.

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:45.460
But on the other, these comments sting
every time.

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:48.345
Why?

00:03:48.345 --> 00:03:54.554
Because it's hypocrisy, and lacks nuance
where nuance is due.

00:03:54.554 --> 00:03:58.952
You see on a personal level, the 130
million gallons that spilled out

00:03:58.952 --> 00:04:04.520
during the BP oil spill, the thousand
generations worth of Starbucks, is a lot.

00:04:04.520 --> 00:04:07.810
And certainly the destructive power of
that amount of oil is severe.

00:04:08.820 --> 00:04:13.170
But from the perspective of consumption,
what spilled out over three months

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:16.655
is only an eighth of what we consume in a
day in the US.

00:04:16.655 --> 00:04:19.790
An eighth.

00:04:19.790 --> 00:04:23.380
And it's not your neighbors, it's us, you
and I, who are the consumers.

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:26.000
And as consumers our thirst for

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.230
oil is insatiable even when we see the
images on TV.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:37.804
But, we don't like to think of ourselves
as consumers, so oil remains black to us.

00:04:37.804 --> 00:04:40.220
But imagine a world without oil in it.

00:04:40.220 --> 00:04:43.262
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:04:43.262 --> 00:04:44.340
There'd be no asphalt.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:46.860
Some fertilizers wouldn't exist.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:48.387
We'd have no gasoline.

00:04:48.387 --> 00:04:50.720
No jet fuel.

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.660
And that's already a stretch of the
imagination.

00:04:53.990 --> 00:04:55.779
Now try to imagine a world without
plastics.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:05:00.820
Everything from toothbrushes to television
screens has either oil or

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.130
gas as the critical input in its
production process.

00:05:07.000 --> 00:05:10.416
For me a world without oil would probably
be a lot like the late 1800s.

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.240
When oil was this new thing that could
make so much happen, much like smartphones

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:20.480
are now actually, and smartphones driven
by arguably the John D.

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:22.962
Rockefeller of our times, Steve Jobs,

00:05:22.962 --> 00:05:28.220
have revolutionized the way we organize
ourselves and communicate with each other.

00:05:28.220 --> 00:05:32.970
[BLANK_AUDIO]

00:05:32.970 --> 00:05:37.759
Certainly I am very thankful for my i,
iPhone,

00:05:37.759 --> 00:05:40.887
and I'm very dependent on it.

00:05:40.887 --> 00:05:45.512
But getting a phone that's in the hands of
every person on the planet has a price.

00:05:45.512 --> 00:05:48.052
There are environmental issues associated
with the extraction of

00:05:48.052 --> 00:05:49.810
the minerals required.

00:05:49.810 --> 00:05:52.610
And if it's true that Apple's manufacturer
had to hang

00:05:52.610 --> 00:05:56.020
nets below its laborer's dorms to prevent
them from committing suicide,

00:05:56.020 --> 00:05:59.030
that doesn't attest to the great labor
conditions in the electronics industry.

00:06:00.270 --> 00:06:02.330
But are we disgusted when we see the new
iPhone?

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:02.830
No.

00:06:03.960 --> 00:06:06.750
In fact, if we were to meet someone from
Apple at a cocktail party,

00:06:06.750 --> 00:06:10.040
we'd say something along the lines of, oh,
Apple is it?

00:06:10.040 --> 00:06:11.570
I'm big user of Apple products, myself.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.235
But, we never say oh, Exxon is it, I'm a
big user of oil products, myself.

00:06:16.235 --> 00:06:23.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:06:23.480 --> 00:06:28.240
&gt;&gt; My point is, rarely is anything that
truly rocks the foundation of society,

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:32.450
like oil, like smartphones, all good or
all bad.

00:06:32.450 --> 00:06:36.950
And oil, well it's certainly has a dark
side when the risks manifest themselves.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:39.650
It still fuels the economy, literally.

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.630
And it's not so black and white.

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:46.310
For example, I'm pretty sure that you feel
great when you can fill up your car for

00:06:46.310 --> 00:06:48.849
under $2.00 a gallon at the pump.

00:06:49.960 --> 00:06:53.980
And you're not alone because the average
American household will save

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:58.540
around $750 this year because of the drop
in the oil price, and that's great news.

00:07:00.470 --> 00:07:05.170
But, the drop in oil price also endangers
the 9 million jobs in the oil and

00:07:05.170 --> 00:07:06.670
gas industry in the US.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:07.750
That's one in 20 jobs.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:10.320
A similar contradiction?

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:14.780
Reduce dependence on foreign oil is a
prominent highlight that

00:07:14.780 --> 00:07:17.970
any president would love to boast within
their State of the Union.

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:22.282
But in casual conversation, we condemn the
Shell gas boom

00:07:22.282 --> 00:07:26.595
that almost single-handedly got us this
independence.

00:07:26.595 --> 00:07:29.379
Whose side is she on, you might be
thinking.

00:07:29.379 --> 00:07:30.845
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH].

00:07:30.845 --> 00:07:33.740
&gt;&gt; I'm rooting for accuracy and fairness
in debate.

00:07:35.130 --> 00:07:37.570
In a world of highly polarized media, and

00:07:37.570 --> 00:07:41.300
in a community where really taking a
stance is often rewarded,

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:44.840
I invite you to join me on this
uncomfortable space in the middle.

00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:51.540
Let's go all out on renewable energy and
energy efficiency, but

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.420
also recognize that oil is really doing
something for us in the meantime.

00:07:56.555 --> 00:07:59.120
Let's hold the oil industry to really high
standards, but

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:00.520
also respect the people who work in it.

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:07.300
Because the dismissive treatment of oil
just seemed like a form of hypocrisy,

00:08:07.300 --> 00:08:10.980
when most of us don't really walk, our
anti-oil talk.

00:08:12.480 --> 00:08:14.860
Are we really doing all we can to reduce
our oil footprint?

00:08:16.700 --> 00:08:19.270
Things like reducing the amount of plastic
we use and waste.

00:08:20.490 --> 00:08:23.600
Fighting for teleconferencing facilities
at our office instead of going for

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:24.840
those much loved business trips.

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:29.740
Taking holidays in Big Sur rather than
flying off to New York for the weekend.

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:34.820
Opting to buy stuff that hasn't been air
freighted in from the other

00:08:34.820 --> 00:08:36.020
side of the planet.

00:08:36.020 --> 00:08:38.239
Or driving a more efficient car if you
can't get there by bicycle.

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:42.270
But these aren't things that you, know,
not heard before.

00:08:43.690 --> 00:08:47.680
For me, the intermediate, more important
step,

00:08:47.680 --> 00:08:51.600
is that we need to come to terms with our
complex, difficult and

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:54.770
above all ambiguous relationship with oil,
as individual consumers.

00:08:56.570 --> 00:08:59.628
And that is that oil is a substance that
both creates and

00:08:59.628 --> 00:09:02.230
destroys many of the things that we hold
dear.

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:10.083
On complex issues such as these my hope is
that we go from visceral black and

00:09:10.083 --> 00:09:15.137
white reactions, to responding from a
place of full awareness.

00:09:15.137 --> 00:09:18.715
My hope is that the next time you see oil,

00:09:18.715 --> 00:09:24.095
you react as an intrigued consumer and see
oil in full color.

00:09:24.095 --> 00:09:24.845
Thank you.

00:09:24.845 --> 00:09:28.054
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:09:28.054 --> 00:09:32.369
[MUSIC]

